    <h2>2. Purpose & Mathematical Estimation Disclaimer</h2>
    <p>
      PrintPixel is an informational calculation tool intended to assist photographers, graphic artists, and consumers in estimating physical print dimensions based on digital pixel dimensions and dots-per-inch (DPI) densities.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Calculations are mathematical estimations based on optical standards:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>300 DPI</strong>: Industry benchmark for fine-art, photographic, and handheld media viewed at close distance (~10–12 inches).</li>
      <li><strong>150 DPI</strong>: Common standard for magazine spreads, posters, and canvas wraps viewed at arm's length (~2–3 feet).</li>
      <li><strong>72 DPI</strong>: Traditional standard for large-format displays, billboards, and signage viewed from several meters away.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr />

    <h2>3. Proof Prints & Critical Commercial Work</h2>
    <p>
      Physical print fidelity is influenced by numerous external factors beyond raw pixel counts, including but not limited to:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Printer hardware resolution and Raster Image Processor (RIP) rendering algorithms</li>
      <li>Substrate characteristics (e.g., matte versus glossy paper, canvas texture, ink absorption rate)</li>
      <li>Color space profiles (RGB to CMYK conversion) and lighting conditions</li>
      <li>Original camera lens sharpness, optical blur, and digital sensor noise</li>
    </ul>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>Commercial Advisory:</strong> For critical client deliverables, large print runs, gallery exhibits, or high-value commercial jobs, <strong>always order a physical proof print</strong> from your lab before committing to final production.
    </blockquote>

